:\n              - /url: /wiki/Amin%C3%A9\n            - text: .\n          - figure \"Primary amine\" [ref=e99]:\n            - link [ref=e100] [cursor=pointer]:\n              - /url: /wiki/File:Amine_Functional_Group.png\n              - img [ref=e101]\n            - generic [ref=e102]: Primary amine\n          - paragraph [ref=e103]:\n            - text: In\n            - link \"chemistry\" [ref=e104] [cursor=pointer]:\n              - /url: /wiki/Chemistry\n            - text: \", amines (\"\n            - link /əˈmiːn, ˈæmiːn/ [ref=e107] [cursor=pointer]:\n              - /url: /wiki/Help:IPA/English\n              - text: /\n              - generic [ref=e108]: əˈmiːn\n              - text: \",\"\n              - generic [ref=e109]: ˈæmiːn\n              - text: /\n            - text: \",\"\n            - superscript [ref=e110]:\n              - link \"[1]\" [ref=e111] [cursor=pointer]:\n                - /url: \"#cite_note-1\"\n            - superscript [ref=e112]:\n              - link \"[2]\" [ref=e113] [cursor=pointer]:\n                - /url: \"#cite_note-2\"\n            - text: UK also\n            - link /ˈeɪmiːn/ [ref=e116] [cursor=pointer]:\n              - /url: /wiki/Help:IPA/English\n              - text: /\n              - generic [ref=e117]: ˈeɪmiːn\n              - text: /\n            - superscript [ref=e118]:\n              - link \"[3]\" [ref=e119] [cursor=pointer]:\n                - /url: \"#cite_note-3\"\n            - text: ) are\n            - link \"organic compounds\" [ref=e120] [cursor=pointer]:\n              - /url: /wiki/Organic_compound\n            - text: that contain carbon–nitrogen bonds.\n            - superscript [ref=e121]:\n              - link \"[4]\" [ref=e122] [cursor=pointer]:\n                - /url: \"#cite_note-4\"\n            - text: Amines are formed when one or more hydrogen atoms in\n            - link \"ammonia\" [ref=e123] [cursor=pointer]:\n              - /url: /wiki/Ammonia\n            - text: are replaced by alkyl or aryl groups.\n            - superscript [ref=e124]:\n              - link \"[5]\" [ref=e125] [cursor=pointer]:\n                - /url: \"#cite_note-5\"\n            - text: The nitrogen atom in an amine possesses a lone pair of electrons. Amines can also exist as heterocyclic compounds. Aniline\n            - generic [ref=e126]:\n              - text: (\n              - generic [ref=e127]:\n                - math [ref=e129]:\n                  - generic [ref=e133]:\n                    - generic [ref=e134]:\n                      - generic [ref=e135]: C\n                      - generic [ref=e137]: \"6\"\n                    - generic [ref=e138]:\n                      - generic [ref=e139]: H\n                      - generic [ref=e141]: \"7\"\n                    - generic [ref=e142]: \"N\"\n                - img [ref=e143]\n              - text: )\n            - text: is the simplest aromatic amine, consisting of a benzene ring bonded to an amino\n            - generic [ref=e144]:\n              - text: (\n              - generic [ref=e145]:\n                - math [ref=e147]:\n                  - generic [ref=e151]:\n                    - generic [ref=e153]: −\n                    - generic [ref=e154]:\n                      - generic [ref=e155]: NH\n                      - generic [ref=e157]: \"2\"\n                - img [ref=e158]\n              - text: )\n            - text: group.\n            - superscript [ref=e159]:\n              - link \"[6]\" [ref=e160] [cursor=pointer]:\n                - /url: \"#cite_note-6\"\n            - superscript [ref=e161]:\n              - link \"[7]\" [ref=e162] [cursor=pointer]:\n                - /url: \"#cite_note-Ullmann-7\"\n          - figure \"3D diagram of the aromatic compound of aniline. The light black balls are carbon atoms, and white balls are hydrogen atoms, while the nitrogen atom is the blue ball. The dots in carbon bonds represent localization of electrons.\" [ref=e163]:\n            - link [ref=e164] [cursor=pointer]:\n              - /url: /wiki/File:Aniline-3D-balls.png\n              - img [ref=e165]\n            - generic [ref=e166]: 3D diagram of the aromatic compound of aniline. The light black balls are carbon atoms, and white balls are hydrogen atoms, while the nitrogen atom is the blue ball. The dots in carbon bonds represent localization of electrons.\n          - paragraph [ref=e167]:\n            - text: \"Amines are classified into three types: primary (1°), secondary (2°), and tertiary (3°) amines. Primary amines (1°) contain one alkyl or aryl substituent and have the general formula\"\n            - generic [ref=e168]:\n              - math [ref=e170]:\n                - generic [ref=e175]:\n                  - generic [ref=e176]: RNH\n                  - generic [ref=e178]: \"2\"\n              - img [ref=e179]\n            - text: . Secondary amines (2°) have two alkyl or aryl groups attached to the nitrogen atom, with the general formula\n            - generic [ref=e180]:\n              - math [ref=e182]:\n                - generic [ref=e186]:\n                  - generic [ref=e187]:\n                    - generic [ref=e188]: R\n                    - generic [ref=e190]: \"2\"\n                  - generic [ref=e191]: NH\n              - img [ref=e192]\n            - text: . Tertiary amines (3°) contain three substituent groups bonded to the nitrogen atom, and are represented by the formula\n            - generic [ref=e193]:\n              - math [ref=e195]:\n                - generic [ref=e199]:\n                  - generic [ref=e200]:\n                    - generic [ref=e201]: R\n                    - generic [ref=e203]: \"3\"\n                  - generic [ref=e204]: \"N\"\n              - img [ref=e205]\n            - text: .\n            - superscript [ref=e206]:\n              - link \"[8]\" [ref=e207] [cursor=pointer]:\n                - /url: \"#cite_note-8\"\n          - paragraph [ref=e208]:\n            - text: The\n            - link \"functional group\" [ref=e209] [cursor=pointer]:\n              - /url: /wiki/Functional_group\n            - generic [ref=e210]:\n              - text: −NH\n              - subscript [ref=e211]: \"2\"\n            - text: present in primary amines is called the amino group.\n            - superscript [ref=e212]:\n              - link \"[9]\" [ref=e213] [cursor=pointer]:\n                - /url: \"#cite_note-OChemSmith3E-9\"\n          - generic [ref=e214]:\n            - heading \"Classification of amines\" [level=2] [ref=e215]\n            - generic [ref=e216]:\n              - text: \"[\"\n              - link \"edit\" [ref=e217] [cursor=pointer]:\n                - /url: /w/index.php?title=Amine&action=edit&section=1\n              - text: \"]\"\n          - paragraph [ref=e218]:\n            - text: Amines can be classified according to the nature and number of substituents on\n            - link \"nitrogen\" [ref=e219] [cursor=pointer]:\n              - /url: /wiki/Nitrogen\n            - text: . Aliphatic amines contain only H and alkyl substituents.\n            - link \"Aromatic amines\" [ref=e221] [cursor=pointer]:\n              - /url: /wiki/Aromatic_amine\n            - text: have the nitrogen atom connected to an\n            - link \"aromatic\" [ref=e222] [cursor=pointer]:\n              - /url: /wiki/Aromaticity\n            - text: ring.\n          - table [ref=e223]:\n            - rowgroup [ref=e224]:\n              - row \"Primary (1°) amine Secondary (2°) amine Tertiary (3°) amine\" [ref=e225]:\n                - columnheader \"Primary (1°) amine\" [ref=e226]\n                - columnheader \"Secondary (2°) amine\" [ref=e227]\n                - columnheader \"Tertiary (3°) amine\" [ref=e228]\n              - row \"primary amine secondary amine tertiary amine\" [ref=e229]:\n                - cell \"primary amine\" [ref=e230]:\n                  - link \"primary amine\" [ref=e232] [cursor=pointer]:\n                    - /url: /wiki/File:Primary-amine-2D-general.svg\n                    - img \"primary amine\" [ref=e233]\n                - cell \"secondary amine\" [ref=e234]:\n                  - link \"secondary amine\" [ref=e236] [cursor=pointer]:\n                    - /url: /wiki/File:Secondary-amine-2D-general.svg\n                    - img \"secondary amine\" [ref=e237]\n                - cell \"tertiary amine\" [ref=e238]:\n                  - link \"tertiary amine\" [ref=e240] [cursor=pointer]:\n                    - /url: /wiki/File:Amine-2D-general.svg\n                    - img \"tertiary amine\" [ref=e241]\n          - paragraph [ref=e242]:\n            - text: Amines, alkyl and aryl alike, are organized into three subcategories\n            - link \"(see table)\" [ref=e243] [cursor=pointer]:\n              - /url: \"#amino_group_connectivity_table\"\n            - text: based on the number of\n            - link \"carbon\" [ref=e244] [cursor=pointer]:\n              - /url: /wiki/Carbon\n            - text: atoms adjacent to the nitrogen (how many hydrogen atoms of the\n            - link \"ammonia\" [ref=e245] [cursor=pointer]:\n              - /url: /wiki/Ammonia\n            - text: molecule are replaced by\n            - link \"hydrocarbon\" [ref=e246] [cursor=pointer]:\n              - /url: /wiki/Hydrocarbon\n            - text: \"groups):\"\n            - superscript [ref=e247]:\n              - link \"[9]\" [ref=e248] [cursor=pointer]:\n                - /url: \"#cite_note-OChemSmith3E-9\"\n            - superscript [ref=e249]:\n              - link \"[10]\" [ref=e250] [cursor=pointer]:\n                - /url: \"#cite_note-Lawrence-10\"\n          - list [ref=e251]:\n            - listitem [ref=e252]:\n              - text: Primary (1°) amines—Primary amines arise when one of three hydrogen atoms in\n              - link \"ammonia\" [ref=e253] [cursor=pointer]:\n                - /url: /wiki/Ammonia\n              - text: is replaced by an\n              - link \"alkyl\" [ref=e254] [cursor=pointer]:\n                - /url: /wiki/Alkyl\n              - text: or\n              - link \"aromatic\" [ref=e255] [cursor=pointer]:\n                - /url: /wiki/Aromatic\n              - text: group. Important primary alkyl amines include\n              - link \"methylamine\" [ref=e256] [cursor=pointer]:\n                - /url: /wiki/Methylamine\n              - text: \", most\"\n              - link \"amino acids\" [ref=e257] [cursor=pointer]:\n                - /url: /wiki/Amino_acid\n              - text: \", and the\"\n              - link \"buffering agent\" [ref=e258] [cursor=pointer]:\n                - /url: /wiki/Buffering_agent\n              - link \"tris\" [ref=e259] [cursor=pointer]:\n                - /url: /wiki/Tris\n              - text: \", while primary aromatic amines include\"\n              - link \"aniline\" [ref=e260] [cursor=pointer]:\n                - /url: /wiki/Aniline\n              - text: .\n              - superscript [ref=e261]:\n                - link \"[11]\" [ref=e262] [cursor=pointer]:\n                  - /url: \"#cite_note-:0-11\"\n            - listitem [ref=e263]:\n              - text: Secondary (2°) amines—Secondary amines have two organic substituents (alkyl, aryl or both) bound to the nitrogen together with one hydrogen. Important representatives include\n              - link \"dimethylamine\" [ref=e264] [cursor=pointer]:\n                - /url: /wiki/Dimethylamine\n              - text: \", while an example of an\"\n              - link \"aromatic amine\" [ref=e265] [cursor=pointer]:\n                - /url: /wiki/Aromatic_amine\n              - text: would be\n              - link \"diphenylamine\" [ref=e266] [cursor=pointer]:\n                - /url: /wiki/Diphenylamine\n              - text: .\n              - superscript [ref=e267]:\n                - link \"[11]\" [ref=e268] [cursor=pointer]:\n                  - /url: \"#cite_note-:0-11\"\n            - listitem [ref=e269]:\n              - text: Tertiary (3°) amines—In tertiary amines, nitrogen has three organic substituents. Examples include\n              - link \"trimethylamine\" [ref=e270] [cursor=pointer]:\n                - /url: /wiki/Trimethylamine\n              - text: \", which has a distinctively fishy smell, and\"\n              - link \"EDTA\" [ref=e271] [cursor=pointer]:\n                - /url: /wiki/EDTA\n              - text: .\n              - superscript [ref=e272]:\n                - link \"[11]\" [ref=e273] [cursor=pointer]:\n                  - /url: \"#cite_note-:0-11\"\n          - paragraph [ref=e274]: \"A fourth subcategory is determined by the connectivity of the substituents attached to the nitrogen:\"\n          - list [ref=e275]:\n            - listitem [ref=e276]:\n              - text: Cyclic amines—\n              - link \"Cyclic\" [ref=e277] [cursor=pointer]:\n                - /url: /wiki/Cyclic_compound\n              - text: amines are either secondary or tertiary amines. Examples of cyclic amines include the 3-membered ring\n              - link \"aziridine\" [ref=e278] [cursor=pointer]:\n                - /url: /wiki/Aziridine\n              - text: and the six-membered ring\n              - link \"piperidine\" [ref=e279] [cursor=pointer]:\n                - /url: /wiki/Piperidine\n              - text: . N-methylpiperidine and N-phenylpiperidine are examples of cyclic tertiary amines.\n              - superscript [ref=e280]:\n                - link \"[10]\" [ref=e281] [cursor=pointer]:\n                  - /url: \"#cite_note-Lawrence-10\"\n          - generic [ref=e282]:\n            - heading \"Related compounds\" [level=3] [ref=e283]\n            - generic [ref=e284]:\n              - text: \"[\"\n              - link \"edit\" [ref=e285] [cursor=pointer]:\n                - /url: /w/index.php?title=Amine&action=edit&section=2\n              - text: \"]\"\n          - paragraph [ref=e286]:\n            - text: Compounds where the\n            - link \"nitrogen\" [ref=e287] [cursor=pointer]:\n              - /url: /wiki/Nitrogen\n            - text: atom is attached to a\n            - link \"carbonyl group\" [ref=e288] [cursor=pointer]:\n              - /url: /wiki/Carbonyl_group\n            - text: \", thus having the structure R−C(=O)−NR′R″, are called\"\n            - link \"amides\" [ref=e289] [cursor=pointer]:\n              - /url: /wiki/Amide\n            - text: . Compounds containing four organic substituents on the nitrogen, thus having the structure\n            - generic [ref=e290]:\n              - text: R\n              - subscript [ref=e291]: \"4\"\n              - text: \"N\"\n              - superscript [ref=e292]: +\n              - text: X\n              - superscript [ref=e293]: −\n            - text: \", are\"\n            - link \"quaternary ammonium salts\" [ref=e294] [cursor=pointer]:\n              - /url: /wiki/Quaternary_ammonium_salts\n            - text: . Many kinds of\n            - link \"anions\" [ref=e295] [cursor=pointer]:\n              - /url: /wiki/Anions\n            - text: are found in such compounds.\n          - generic [ref=e296]:\n            - heading \"Naming conventions\" [level=2] [ref=e297]\n            - generic [ref=e298]:\n              - text: \"[\"\n              - link \"edit\" [ref=e299] [cursor=pointer]:\n                - /url: /w/index.php?title=Amine&action=edit&section=3\n              - text: \"]\"\n          - paragraph [ref=e300]:\n            - text: Amines are named in several ways. Typically, the compound is given the prefix \"amino-\" or the suffix \"-amine\". The prefix \"N-\" shows substitution on the nitrogen\n\n[...TRUNCATED - page too large]\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT>>>"}],"details":{"ok":true,"format":"ai","targetId":"CD1F17EDDF4041EAA0B1C76DCD7693A3","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amine#Classification_of_amines","truncated":true,"refs":243,"externalContent":{"untrusted":true,"source":"browser","kind":"snapshot","format":"ai","wrapp